WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
Christmas!

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:07.540
That magical time of year - crackling log
fires, freshly fallen snow, twinkling lights,

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:13.140
and murderous monster cats.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:17.260
Krampus isn't the only Christmas monster. So is this!

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:21.580
Well, a much larger version of this known
as the Yule Cat.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:27.460
But don't be fooled...while it may be fluffy,
unlike this kitty, the Yule Cat is no cuddly creature.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.300
Called the Jólakötturinn
from the Icelandic world for jól

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:37.890
+&lrm; köttur this creepy kitty
prowls the streets looking for its prey—anyone

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.930
who has not received new clothes by Christmas.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:47.590
But Iceland has no wild large, predatory cat
species, so why does this creature take on

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:48.899
a feline appearance?

00:00:48.899 --> 00:00:54.160
And why do these monstrous fashion critics
only attack people with old clothes?

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.809
To find out we have to take a look at Icelandic
mythology, the importance of oral storytelling,

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:00.289
and wool production.

00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:02.190
Also…murder floofs.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:17.170
With glowing eyes and sharp whiskers, the
Yule Cat towers over houses as it prowls the

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:20.640
snowy streets hunting people rather than mice.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.240
The Yule Cat’s favorite prey is children.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:28.480
The giant feline lurks along snowy streets
and countrysides, checking in windows to see

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.420
which children received new clothes for Christmas.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:37.460
If the Yule Cat spots anyone with frayed or
torn clothing, he eats them.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:41.580
Perhaps the most famous account of the Yule
Cat and the one most modern Icelanders are

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.120
familiar with, comes from a poem.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.090
Björk even did a cover of it.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:51.060
From the book Christmas is Coming first published
in 1932, but translated to English for the

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.840
first time in 2015, the author writes:

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.490
“He opened his glaring eyes wide, The two
of them glowing bright. It took a really brave

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:05.840
man To look straight into them./ His whiskers,
sharp as bristles, His back arched up high. And

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.470
the claws of his hairy paws/Were a terrible
sight. He gave a wave of his strong tail, He

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:16.659
jumped and he clawed and he hissed Sometimes
up in the valley Sometimes down by the shore. He

00:02:16.659 --> 00:02:23.010
roamed at large, hungry and evil/In the freezing,
Yule snow In every home, People shuddered at

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:28.709
his name, If one heard a pitiful ‘meow’, Something
evil would happen soon. Everybody knew he

00:02:28.709 --> 00:02:31.790
hunted men, But didn’t care for mice.”

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:35.040
Doesn’t that give you the warm fuzzies?

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.390
Before we dive into the possible reasons why
this monster exists, we first must position

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.900
him as part of a larger tradition.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.840
Where does this Yule Cat come from?

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:49.859
Legend and literary history tell us that he
is actually the pet of a family of trolls,

00:02:49.859 --> 00:02:50.990
including the Yule Lads.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:54.519
Iceland does not actually have a Santa Claus.

00:02:54.519 --> 00:02:58.659
The Yule Lads, or Christmas Lads, serve as
the gift-givers children receive presents

00:02:58.659 --> 00:02:59.819
from every year.

00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:05.790
While the number of lads ranges from 9 to
22, the most common number is 13.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:10.230
Since around the 17th century, these trolls
came down from their mountain homes one by

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:15.909
one over a period of 13 days before Christmas
to leave small presents—and cause a little

00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:17.379
mischief along the way.

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:21.470
The brothers have very odd names like “Stubby”
 Window-Peeper”

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:22.999
and “Spoon-licker”.

00:03:22.999 --> 00:03:27.739
In the 20th century the Yule Lads adopted
red clothing and white beards, inspired by

00:03:27.739 --> 00:03:29.549
the Santa Claus from other cultures.

00:03:29.549 --> 00:03:34.090
But, these seemingly harmless trolls were
not always so friendly.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:37.730
Originally it was said that they came down from the mountains not to spread Christmas

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.359
cheer, but to terrify children into obedience.

00:03:41.359 --> 00:03:44.290
They carried off the ones who misbehaved or cried too much.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.909
This was a successful enough deterrent that it actually caused a bit of panic.

00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:54.739
Enough so that in 1764 a regulation was issued from the government saying “The foolish

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:59.079
custom, which has been practiced here and
there about the country, of scaring children

00:03:59.079 --> 00:04:02.529
with Yuletide Lads or ghosts, shall be abolished.”

00:04:02.529 --> 00:04:07.779
I should also mention that the Yule Lads have quite the famous mother— Grýla who has

00:04:07.779 --> 00:04:11.639
been around since at least the early 13th century.

00:04:11.639 --> 00:04:17.390
Early versions describe the troll with 300
heads, fifteen tails, goat horns, long ears,

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:18.410
and a beard.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:22.320
Add in the Yule Cat and it’s just one big,
happy, wicked family.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:28.360
I had the privilege of speaking to the foremost authority in Iceland on Yule tradition,

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:33.970
Árni Björnsson, who told me pinpointing the exact origin of the Yule Cat is a particularly slippery problem.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:39.370
According to him, scholars don’t know if
this creature was widely known in the country

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:44.350
before the first edition of Iceland’s folktales were published in the 19th century.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:49.910
But, while the Yule Cat may have an indeterminate history, there are some compelling theories

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:51.500
as to why the creature exists.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:56.320
I think part of the reason lies in the fact
that cats have always been more common than

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.390
dogs in Iceland, both as pets and wild animals.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:05.840
Dogs were even banned from the country in 1924, and dog owners today must obtain a special

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.480
permit to keep a canine companion.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:11.780
Cats also served an important function in
Icelandic mythology.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:17.960
The goddess of love and sorcery, Freya, traveled across the sky in a wagon pulled by two giant, flying cats.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.280
Specifically, cats resembling the Norwegian forest cat.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:28.860
I’m not much of a cat person, but even I
am smitten by these large, extra fluffy kitties.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:33.500
The Norwegian forest cat’s prevalence in
mythology (and as housepets) may account for

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.350
the Yule Cat’s shaggy appearance.

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:40.440
Dr. Björnsson, along with other scholars,
propose that the creation of the Yule Cat

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.630
may also have been influenced by the Norwegian Yule Goat.

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:49.690
Like the Yule Cat, this mythical creature
is also associated with a gift-giving entity,

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:51.130
this time Saint Nicholas.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:56.590
Saint Nick was said to ride on a goat or parade a large one around a chain on while delivering

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.470
presents to symbolize his power over the devil.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:05.190
Then there’s the possibility that another
Icelandic feline legend played a role—the

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:09.880
waste-land cat or ghoul-cat. Larger and more ferocious than regular

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:15.200
cats, these monsters bury themselves in the ground, preferably in a cemetery, at an early age.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.240
It can grow to the size of a large dog before springing from the ground to roam the rocky

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:23.690
terrain attacking sheep, birds, and humans.

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:28.060
As the story goes, the waste-land cat’s
powerful gaze can kill a human outright.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:34.610
Goats, St. Nick, a goddess, the devil, and
yet another cat with a taste for human flesh—that’s

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:36.110
a lot to consider.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:41.030
But the Yule Cat’s connection to clothing
however, reveals an interesting social aspect

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:42.270
to Icelandic culture.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:47.300
Most scholars, myself included, agree that the threat of the Yule Cat represents a rather

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:52.430
practical problem—the need for warm clothes and a successful wool production.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:56.920
Iceland began exporting wool in the Middle Ages, and it became vital to the Icelandic

00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:59.470
economy and survival.

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:04.930
During this time, wool related chores were
the responsibility of the entire household.

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:09.530
After the food preparations for winter were completed in autumn, the largest tasks at

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:11.940
hand were spinning and weaving.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:16.110
Casual workers and boarders in a homestead were often paid in wool.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:20.650
Icelandic sheep’s wool has two kinds of
hair: a coarse, more water-resistant top layer

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:23.520
] and the inner insulating layer.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.420
These would be separated by hand and used for different textiles.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:31.840
Men, women, and children would tease, card, spin, and knit the wool in order to produce

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:32.840
yarn to sell.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:35.110
They would also knit garments for winter.

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:39.841
In the past, Christmas gifts among most people were sparse and usually consisted of some

00:07:39.841 --> 00:07:45.280
small piece of clothing---but that was only
possible if everyone had participated in the

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:49.090
annual production of wool and done their fair share of the work.

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:54.080
Because the Yule Cat only attacks people who do not receive new clothes, this monster likely

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:59.060
emerged as a response to the importance of wool production, scaring children (and even

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:03.200
adults) into finishing their textile work
in time for Christmas and the coldest part

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:04.610
of the year.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:09.680
The tradition of the Yule Cat promoted a strong work ethic and appropriate preparation for winter.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:14.140
But it also helped encourage familial and
social bonding.

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:19.310
Gathering the household together around the fire on winter nights to work on wool production

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:21.810
served an educational function.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:26.550
Iceland had almost total universal literacy
in the nineteenth century including in the

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:31.580
lower classes even without a “significant”
educational infrastructure.

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:34.829
Children learned to read and write while these chores took place.

00:08:34.829 --> 00:08:38.479
After children learned the alphabet, they
were instructed to read out loud to entertain

00:08:38.479 --> 00:08:41.740
the older participants and improve their own literacy.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:46.460
Frequently these stories included elves, trolls, and other supernatural beings.

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:51.850
What was once a ferocious warning against laziness, is now just a quirky part of Iceland’s

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.769
Yule traditions.

00:08:53.769 --> 00:08:58.850
In 2018 the City of Reykjavík installed a
five meter high, six meter long LED sculpture

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:03.800
of the Yule Cat, it’s glowing red eyes serving as a playful reminder of the importance of

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:07.540
hard work, and as a display of the power of storytelling.

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:13.509
The great cost of the statue caused controversy, but nonetheless it continues to attract crowds.

00:09:13.509 --> 00:09:18.670
Every year around Christmas, Bjöork’s famous musical rendition of the 1932 Yule Cat poem

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.300
can be heard on radio stations across Iceland.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:24.779
The famous feline inspires books, holiday greeting

00:09:24.779 --> 00:09:26.920
cards, and even chocolate.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:31.520
The Yule Cat legend shows us how Icelandic culture emphasized the importance of hard winter.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.980
It served as a warning to young children that being lazy would have serious consequences,

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:42.370
not the least of which included being clawed and eaten alive by a massive cat.

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:45.280
So may your days be merry and bright.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:50.140
And may all your Christmases be free of giant, flesh-eating murder floofs.

